  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nA pattern shift will occur during the mid to late part of the week. \nThe upper-level ridge will shift eastward and become oriented from \nTexas to the Lower Mississippi Valley and Gulf Coast states as an \nupper-level trough develops along the western CONUS. Our forecast \narea will still remain under the influence of the western periphery \nof the ridge through the end of the week, so day time temperatures \nwill remain hot with highs continuing to average above normal in the \nupper 90s to around 103 degrees over much of the area, except lower \nto mid 90s in the mountains and up to 105-110 degrees along the Rio \nGrande. Boundary layer moisture does look to increase over western \nportions of our region on the western side of the ridge axis \nWednesday-Friday, so there may be an uptick in convective coverage \nover the western zones each day. The best chance of daily \nshowers/storms (30-50%) will be over the typical higher terrain \nareas of the Guadalupe/Davis Mountains and Marfa Plateau, though \nat least isolated (10-20% chance) storms may develop from the \nwestern Permian Basin to the Upper Trans Pecos and Big Bend by \nThursday and Friday. The rest of the Permian Basin and the Lower \nTrans Pecos is currently forecast to stay dry where subsidence \nwill be greater in closer proximity to the upper ridge. The ridge \nstill looks to retrograde westward over much of the southern CONUS\nthis weekend. Isolated to scattered afternoon storms should \nbecome more relegated to the higher terrain areas Saturday and \nSunday. Temperatures will remain slightly above normal with \nreadings in the mid 90s to around 102 for most locations through \nthe weekend.",
